Flexibility? Reporting barriers? That sounds like a recipe for bureaucratic overload and endless excuses. I don't disagree with flexibility, but it needs to have rigid standards to adhere to.&#x20;

Look, the military runs on structure and accountability. If you don't meet the standards, there are consequences. Compassion is important, but it can't come at the expense of effectiveness and order. We need a UBI system that's just as disciplined. The structure needs to be rigid, and only be adjusted for specific cases that will be determined on a case by case basis.

Instead of open-ended \"reporting,\" let's talk about clear metrics and benchmarks. Set clear expectations for participation in job training or community service, and then have a system for verifying compliance. If someone consistently fails to meet those benchmarks, then, and only then, do we consider reducing or suspending their UBI. It has to be results-driven, not feelings-driven.
